{#-
  PASS 2 — user prompt.

  Rendered once for the whole label pool, or once per shard on a corpus too
  large to fit in one call (`sharded` is then true and the shard carries the
  most frequent labels as shared context, so the partial taxonomies come back in
  comparable terms).

  Available here: main_topics, subtopics, methods (pre-rendered "- label (n)"
  blocks), n_papers, min_areas, max_areas, min_area_papers, max_area_papers,
  seed, sharded, project.
-#}
These labels were produced while reading the {{ n_papers }} papers of
{{ project }}. Each line is a label and the number of papers it came from.
{% if sharded %}

This is one slice of a larger label pool, prefixed by the pool's most frequent
labels so that your areas are drawn in the same terms as the other slices'.
Cover this slice; do not worry about what is missing from it.
{% endif %}

MAIN TOPICS
{{ main_topics }}

SUBTOPICS
{{ subtopics }}

METHOD FAMILIES
{{ methods }}
{% if seed %}

REQUIRED AREAS

These areas must appear in your taxonomy. Keep each name exactly as written.
Refine a definition if the labels show it should cover more or less than stated,
and give each one subtopics drawn from the labels above. Then induce the
remaining areas around them, covering everything these do not.
{% for area in seed.areas %}

- {{ area.name }}{% if area.definition %}: {{ area.definition }}{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{% endif %}

Organise this into a canonical taxonomy of {{ min_areas }}-{{ max_areas }} main
areas.

Requirements:
- Every area should plausibly cover between {{ min_area_papers }} and
  {{ max_area_papers }} of the {{ n_papers }} papers. Nothing should be so broad
  that it swallows the corpus, or so narrow that it names a single paper.
- Give each area 2-8 subtopics drawn from, or generalising, the labels above.
- Area names are 2-5 words, in your field's standard terminology, and read well
  as a session title.
- definition: one sentence saying what belongs in the area and what does not.
- absorbs: the raw labels above that this area covers. This is how the mapping
  is checked, so list them generously.
- Together the areas must cover every label. Add a final catch-all area only if
  genuinely necessary.
